  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical findings are based on a qualitative case study of a single, highly stigmatized port city, which may limit direct generalizability to cities with different political or economic structures.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the discursive production of territorial stigma in Callao, Peru, by examining press media coverage, state-of-emergency decrees, institutional archives, and semi-structured interviews with local residents. It traces how the Peruvian state and media frame specific urban slums as dangerous, introducing the concept of the biopolitical enemy to explain how certain populations are subjected to governance through states of exception.",
      "why": "It demonstrates how state-led security discourses and media stigmatization are directly linked to urban renewal dynamics, showing that the construction of a territorial enemy is actively used to justify state control and physical restructuring in marginalized neighborhoods."
    }
  },
